  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Les Esclamandes beach, on the way out of Saint-Aygulf towards Fréjus, is split into four zones: family, naturist, kitesurf, and dogs. The dog zone, called Argens beach, occupies the northern end, near the Villepey ponds. Some consider it one of the most dog-friendly beaches in the region, with no time restriction year-round.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Our sources contradict each other on one important point: several general-interest guides present this beach as accessible to dogs on leash all day, year-round, with no time restriction. A more specialist source states instead a restricted permission of before 9am and after 7pm, from 1 May to 30 September, near the lifeguard post — with a slightly different location (between beacons no. 5-6 and no. 23-24, rather than the northern end). The city of Fréjus has an official page dedicated to this ("beaches for dogs") whose exact content we were unable to extract: it's best to check it directly before planning a summer visit. Either way, remember to bring a sunshade, water and poop bags — little equipment is available on site.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the Villepey ponds right behind are a pleasant natural site to explore alongside a swim.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Plage du Pacha, at the other end of the Fréjus Base Nature, is another calm option that allows dogs year-round, with swimming permitted.</p>
    </div>

